  One thing worth saying plainly before the options: if the auth server is the thing assigning [38;5;153muserId[39m, then [3mit already [0m
  [3mknows a login happened[0m. A client-side "track this login" call re-reports an event the server just processed. That's
  worth weighing — client-side tracking is also the least reliable kind, since ad blockers drop it and anyone can forge
  it from a console.

  - [1mServer-side, inside the auth endpoint[0m — the backend records the login as part of handling it. Most reliable and
    unforgeable, and the client stays a one-line change. Costs: needs backend cooperation, and you can't capture
    client-only context (user agent, referrer, which page they came from) unless you deliberately send it.
  - [1mYour own events endpoint[0m — client POSTs to something like [38;5;153m/events[39m after a successful login. You own the data,
    nothing leaves your infrastructure, and you control the schema. Costs: you build and operate it, and it's
    droppable/forgeable like any client call.
  - [1mThird-party analytics[0m — PostHog, Segment, GA. Dashboards and querying for free on day one. Costs: a script
    dependency and a vendor, user data leaves your infrastructure, and it pulls in consent/privacy obligations you don't
    have today. Swapping vendors later means re-instrumenting.

  [1mMy recommendation: server-side, inside the auth endpoint[0m — for the plain reason that the event you want is already
  happening there, and duplicating it on the client buys you unreliability. Pick one of the client-side options only if
  you don't control that backend, or if you specifically need browser context attached to each login.
